---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const failures = [
  ['Fabrication', 'A fact with no source at all — invented whole.', 'Lowest-frequency, highest-shock. Deterministic source checks catch it cleanly.'],
  ['Citation drift', 'A real source, bent to a number or claim it never made.', 'What you saw on stage. The dangerous one: it survives a careful read.'],
  ['Overconfidence', 'A hedge-free answer to something genuinely uncertain.', 'No confidence signal by default. Calibration and abstention are their own evals.'],
  ['Omission', 'What it left out — the counter-evidence, the caveat, the risk.', 'The hardest to test, because you are checking for an absence.'],
  ['Nondeterminism', 'Same input, different answer tomorrow.', 'Why you check every run, not once. Pin what you can; measure variance on the rest.'],
  ['Sycophancy', 'It agrees with the framing in your prompt.', 'Leading questions produce agreeable, wrong answers. Test with adversarial phrasings.'],
  ['Silent regression', 'A model or prompt change quietly breaks what worked.', 'The reason evals live in CI. A green suite is the only thing that catches it before users do.'],
  ['Prompt injection', 'Text in the input hijacks the system’s instructions.', 'An input-side threat, not an output-quality one — handled by guardrails, not offline evals.'],
];

const taxonomy = [
  ['Groundedness / faithfulness', 'Can every claim name the exact sentence it came from?', 'Shown on stage. Exact-quote + number presence; for open RAG, entailment / NLI or judge with a rubric.'],
  ['Correctness / task success', 'Did it actually do the job that was asked?', 'Needs a reference or a checkable outcome. The clearest signal when you can define it.'],
  ['Completeness / schema', 'Is every required part present, in the required shape?', 'Cheap, deterministic, high-value. Structural validation before anything semantic.'],
  ['Relevance / helpfulness', 'Is the answer on-point and useful, not just true?', 'Fuzzy. Usually rubric + judge or human; the classic place people over-trust a judge.'],
  ['Safety', 'Toxicity, PII leakage, resistance to being manipulated.', 'Classifiers + policy checks; overlaps with runtime guardrails.'],
  ['Robustness / adversarial', 'Does it hold up under hostile or edge-case inputs?', 'A red-team set of nasty cases, run like any other regression suite.'],
  ['Regression', 'Does the same set still pass after every change?', 'Not a metric — a discipline. The saved set is the asset; the pass/fail is the gate.'],
];

const interrogate = [
  ['Make it name the sentence.', 'For every fact, ask for the exact source sentence — not a summary, the sentence. If it can’t produce one, the fact is an assumption.'],
  ['Recompute the number yourself.', 'Any internal figure should be re-derivable from the data. Do the count. Trust the data, never the stated total.'],
  ['Ask what would make it wrong.', 'A system that can’t state its own failure conditions hasn’t reasoned about them. Make it argue against itself.'],
  ['Check the sources are real.', 'Open two of them. Confirm the page exists and says what was claimed. Plausible-looking citations are the easiest thing to fabricate.'],
  ['Run it twice and compare.', 'Same prompt, fresh session. Where the two answers disagree is where the uncertainty is hiding.'],
  ['Look for what’s missing.', 'The counter-argument, the caveat, the do-nothing option. Omission is invisible until you ask for it by name.'],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Evals in Real Life"
  description="Past the one check from the workshop: the kinds of evals, when to trust a model to judge, and the ones you'd run on a real AI analyst."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-10">
    <p class="text-sm font-medium text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 mb-3">
      <a href="/trust/" class="hover:underline">Build an AI Analyst You Can Trust</a> · going deeper
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-6">
      Evals in real life.
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
      The workshop showed one check on one document — the smallest honest version of the idea. This is the rest of
      the map: what actually goes wrong with language models, the kinds of checks that catch it, when you can trust a
      model to grade another model and when you cannot, and the specific evals you would run on the FreshCart analyst.
      The one habit never changes — every claim carries its source, and something goes and checks. The toolkit around
      it grows.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-4 flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3 text-sm">
      <div class="flex-1">
        <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Two ways to read this.</span>
        <span class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400"> The plain text is for anyone who commissions AI work or signs off on it.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flex-1">
        <span class="inline-block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-primary-700 dark:text-primary-300 bg-primary-50 dark:bg-primary-950/40 rounded px-1.5 py-0.5">Build →</span>
        <span class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400"> The blue notes go deeper for whoever engineers it.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 1. Atom -> molecule -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      One check was the atom. Here's the molecule.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-3">
      On stage, one deterministic check ran once against one brief and caught one invented statistic. That is the
      atom of the whole discipline: a claim, a source, and code that goes and verifies. A real system keeps the atom
      and grows in three directions — you check a <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">set</b> of cases, not one;
      you run a <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">suite</b> of checks, not one; and you run them at two moments —
      before you ship, and while it is live.
    </p>
    <div class="build">
      <span class="build-label">Build</span>
      <p>The gate you saw is a single assertion. Production shape: a versioned dataset of representative inputs with
      expected properties, a suite of independent checks scored per-case, a CI job that fails the build on regression,
      and a sampling job in production that scores live traffic. The pass/fail is cheap; the <em>dataset</em> is the
      durable asset — treat it like code, review changes to it, and never let the model under test also author its own
      test cases.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. What goes wrong -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      What actually goes wrong.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-6">
      You cannot check for failures you have not named. These are the ones that reach real decisions. Most are not the
      model "lying" — they are the predictable shape of a system that completes patterns under pressure.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="text-left text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold align-top">Failure</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold align-top">In plain terms</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold align-top">What it takes to catch</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100 dark:divide-gray-800/60 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          {failures.map(([name, plain, tech]) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-white">{name}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top">{plain}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">{tech}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="build">
      <span class="build-label">Build</span>
      <p>Two more that bite in production and rarely in demos: <b>Goodhart</b> — once a metric becomes the target, the
      system optimizes the metric instead of the goal (a groundedness score climbs while answers get evasive); and
      <b>eval-set contamination</b> — test cases leak into prompts, fine-tunes, or few-shot examples and the suite
      goes green for the wrong reason. Rotate held-out cases and keep a private set the pipeline never sees.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. Taxonomy -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      What you can check.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-6">
      "Eval" is not one thing. It is a family of questions, each answered a different way. You rarely need all of them —
      you need the two or three that map to how <em>your</em> system fails.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="text-left text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold align-top">Kind of eval</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold align-top">The question it answers</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold align-top">How it's usually done</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100 dark:divide-gray-800/60 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          {taxonomy.map(([name, q, how]) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-white">{name}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top">{q}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">{how}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. How you check + when each fails -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      How you check — and where each one fails.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-6">
      There are only four ways to grade an answer. The skill is matching the method to the question, and knowing the
      failure mode of the method itself.
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-4">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">1 · Deterministic code</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed text-sm">The check you built. Fetch the source, look for the sentence, recompute the number. Exact, cheap, repeatable, and impossible to talk out of a verdict. <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Fails when</b> the thing you care about can't be reduced to a rule — tone, nuance, "is this actually helpful."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">2 · Reference-based</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed text-sm">Compare the answer to a known-good answer. Clear signal when a gold answer exists. <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Fails when</b> there's no single right answer, or surface wording differs from a correct response.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">3 · A model as the judge</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed text-sm">Use one model to grade another's output against a rubric. Scales to nuance that code can't touch. <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Fails when</b> you trust it blindly — a judge has real biases, and it can be wrong in the same direction as the writer.</p>
        <div class="build mt-3">
          <span class="build-label">Build</span>
          <p>Known biases to design around: <b>position</b> (favors the first or last option), <b>verbosity</b> (favors longer answers), <b>self-preference</b> (favors its own style or family), and <b>correlated error</b> (misses exactly what the generator missed). Mitigations: score against an explicit rubric, not vibes; calibrate the judge against human labels before you trust its numbers; randomize order and run pairwise; use judges to <em>rank and triage</em>, not to gate the safety-critical path. The check that matters most stays deterministic — that is the whole reason the stage gate was code.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">4 · Human review</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed text-sm">A person judges. The ground truth the other three are approximations of. <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Fails when</b> you need it at scale or on every run — humans skim, and experts skim faster. Use it to calibrate the cheap checks, not to be the check.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. Two moments -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      Two moments: before you ship, and while it's live.
    </h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
        <div class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">Offline — before you ship</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">Run the suite against your saved set every time anything changes: a prompt, a model, a tool. A red suite blocks the release. This is where the gate lives on stage.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
        <div class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">Online — while it's live</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">Sample real traffic and score it in production. Watch groundedness over time, not just at launch. The two are different jobs: passing offline does not mean it holds on inputs you never imagined.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="build">
      <span class="build-label">Build</span>
      <p>The move people skip: <b>canary a model upgrade against the frozen suite before you cut over.</b> "Newer model" is not "better on your task" — a swap that improves general benchmarks can quietly regress your groundedness. Diff the suite, don't assume. In production, alert on the trend, not the single bad run.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. Guardrails vs evals -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      Guardrails are not evals.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-3">
      People collapse these two and then wonder why one layer isn't enough. An <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">eval</b> is
      the crash test — offline, measuring quality against a set. A <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">guardrail</b> is the
      seatbelt — a check that runs at request time, on the live input and output, and can block or rewrite before the
      user sees it. You need both. The crash test tells you it's safe to ship; the seatbelt protects the ride you
      didn't test for.
    </p>
    <div class="build">
      <span class="build-label">Build</span>
      <p>Runtime guardrails: input filters (prompt-injection and jailbreak detection, PII scrubbing), output filters
      (toxicity, PII, policy, groundedness-at-inference on retrieved context), and refusal/abstention when confidence
      is low. Evals measure the system in aggregate; guardrails defend the single request. A groundedness check exists
      in both worlds — offline as a suite metric, online as a gate on the response.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. Evals for this analyst -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      The evals you'd actually run on this analyst.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-5">
      Concrete, for the FreshCart brief. Two of these you already watched run.
    </p>
    <ul class="space-y-3">
      {[
        ['Groundedness', 'Every web claim’s exact quote is present in the cited source. Deterministic.', 'shown'],
        ['Numeric recompute', 'Every internal statistic is re-derived from the CSV, never trusted as stated.', 'shown'],
        ['Completeness / schema', 'All five brief sections present; every claim carries a source field.', ''],
        ['Retrieval quality', 'The sources it cited are real, reachable, and on-topic — not plausible-looking fabrications.', ''],
        ['Scope', 'It read only the feedback file and its cited sources — nothing it wasn’t handed.', ''],
        ['Regression set', 'A handful of briefs with known-good outcomes, re-run on every prompt or model change.', ''],
      ].map(([name, desc, tag]) => (
        <li class="flex gap-3 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-4">
          <span class="text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 mt-0.5 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>
          <div>
            <div class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white text-sm">
              {name}
              {tag === 'shown' && <span class="ml-2 text-xs font-medium text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 uppercase tracking-wide">shown on stage</span>}
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mt-0.5">{desc}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. What the gate does NOT catch (centerpiece) -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-primary-200 dark:border-primary-900/60 bg-primary-50/50 dark:bg-primary-950/20 p-6 md:p-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
        What the gate does <em>not</em> catch.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-4">
        The most honest thing in this whole workshop. A perfect groundedness gate catches a fabricated citation. It
        does not catch:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed list-disc ml-5 max-w-2xl">
        <li>A <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">real source bent to the wrong conclusion</b> — every sentence checks out, the argument is still false.</li>
        <li><b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Omitted counter-evidence</b> — the study it didn't cite because it undercut the recommendation.</li>
        <li>A <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">reasoning error</b> — sound facts, invalid inference.</li>
        <li><b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">The question it should have asked</b> and didn't.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mt-4">
        No single eval covers all of these — that is precisely why it is a suite, why you add a human judgment layer
        for the things code can't reach, and why a person still signs. The gate does not replace the human. It
        removes the failure a human is worst at catching — the confident, well-sourced, wrong number — so the human
        can spend judgment on the failures only judgment can catch.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. Interrogate -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      How to interrogate any output.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-6">
      The four questions from the checklist, turned into moves you can make on a single answer — before you build any
      of this. Do them by hand first; automate the ones that repeat.
    </p>
    <ol class="space-y-3">
      {interrogate.map(([q, a], i) => (
        <li class="flex gap-4 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
          <span class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 tabular-nums leading-none shrink-0 w-8">{i + 1}</span>
          <div>
            <div class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">{q}</div>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed text-sm">{a}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- 10. Close -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      The habit is constant. The toolkit grows.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-4">
      Every technique on this page is a way to run the same rule at more scale, against more failure modes, at more
      moments — every claim carries its source, and something goes and checks. Start with the one deterministic check
      that catches your worst failure. Add the next one when a real miss earns it. Reach for a model-as-judge only
      where code can't, and never for the decision that matters most.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Tooling for all of this exists — eval harnesses, tracing and observability, guardrail runtimes — and it changes
      every year. The principle outlives the tool: name your failures, save your set, fail closed, keep the receipt.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a class="btn-solid" href="/trust/">Back to the workshop</a>
      <a class="btn-outline" href="https://github.com/kzaky/freshcart-agent" rel="noopener">Get the repo</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style is:global>
  .build { margin-top: 1rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--color-primary-500); background: var(--color-primary-50); border-radius: 0 0.5rem 0.5rem 0; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; }
  .dark .build { background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-primary-950) 35%, transparent); }
  .build p { font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--color-gray-700); margin: 0; }
  .dark .build p { color: var(--color-gray-300); }
  .build em { font-style: italic; }
  .build-label { display: inline-block; font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--color-primary-700); margin-bottom: 0.35rem; }
  .dark .build-label { color: var(--color-primary-300); }
  .btn-solid { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; border-radius: 0.5rem; background: var(--color-primary-600); color: #fff; padding: 0.625rem 1.25rem; font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 500; text-decoration: none; }
  .btn-solid:hover { background: var(--color-primary-700); }
  .btn-outline { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; border-radius: 0.5rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-gray-300); padding: 0.625rem 1.25rem; font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 500; color: var(--color-gray-900); text-decoration: none; }
  .btn-outline:hover { background: var(--color-gray-50); }
  .dark .btn-outline { border-color: var(--color-gray-700); color: #fff; }
  .dark .btn-outline:hover { background: var(--color-gray-900); }
</style>
